    "text": "16 \n\n**_π_** ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) = ( **_π_** _t_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) _, · · · ,_ **_π_** _t_ + _NT −_ 1( **x** _t_ + _NT −_ 1 _,_ **_ξ_** )), where **u** _t_ = **_π_** _t_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) and **_ξ_** _∈_ Ξ. First, we define the multi-step feedback dynamics and cost. \n\n**Definition VIII.1** (Multi-Step Feedback Dynamics) **.** _The multi-step feedback dynamics is given as p_ ( **x** _t_ + _NT |_ **x** _,_ **_ξ_** ) = � _· · ·_ �� _ti_ =+ _tNT −_ 1 _p_ ( **x** _i_ +1 _|_ **x** _i,_ **_π_** _i_ ( **x** _i,_ **_ξ_** )) _d_ **x** _t_ +1 _· · · d_ **x** _t_ + _NT −_ 1 _._ **Definition VIII.2** (Multi-Step Feedback Cost) **.** _We define_ \n\n\n\n\n\n_as the multi-step feedback cost, with analogous definitions for LN_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) _, ℓ_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) _, CN_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) _, and c_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) _._ \n\nGiven these, we show that there exists a feasible multi-step feedback policy within the neighborhood of an optimal multistep feedback policy that descends the value function. \n\n**Theorem VIII.2.** _Under the assumptions of Theorem VIII.1 and a given_ **x** _t, for a large enough γc, if_ Ξ<sup>_c_</sup> _ϵ_<sup>=</sup><sup>_{_</sup><sup>**_ξ_**</sup><sup>_|_</sup><sup>**_π_**</sup><sup>_i_(</sup><sup>**x**</sup><sup>_i,_</sup><sup>**_ξ_**)</sup><sup>_∈_</sup> _U_<sup>_c_</sup> ( **x** _i_ ) _∀ i ∈{t, · · · , t_ + _NT }, V_ **_π_**<sup>_c_</sup> _t_ ( _·,_ **_ξ_** )<sup>(</sup><sup>**x**</sup><sup>_t_)</sup><sup>_< ϵ}̸_= ∅</sup><sup>_,where_</sup> Ξ _is compact,_ **_π_** _depends continuously on_ **_ξ_** _, and the policy parameterization is rich enough to represent the optimal multistep feedback policy,_ **_π_** ( _·,_ **_ξ_**<sup>_∗_</sup> ) = ( **_π_**<sup>_∗_</sup> _t_<sup>_, · · ·,_</sup><sup>**_π_**</sup><sup>_∗_</sup> _t_ + _NT −_ 1<sup>)</sup><sup>_,then_</sup> _there exist feasible policy parameters_ **_ξ_** _c in the neighborhood of_ **_ξ_**<sup>_∗_</sup> _that satisfies c_ ( **x** _i,_ **_π_** _i_ ( **x** _i,_ **_ξ_** _c_ )) = 0 _∀ i ∈{t, ..., t_ + _NT −_ 1 _} and_ ∆ _V_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** _c_ ) = _β_ E [ _V_ ( **x** _t_ + _NT_ ) _|_ **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** _c_ ] _− V_ ( **x** _t_ ) _<_ 0 _._ \n\n_Proof._ We show that the assumptions necessary for Theorem VIII.1 apply not only to the single-step dynamics and cost, but also to the multi-step versions. \n\nIt follows from Definitions VIII.1 and VIII.2 and the assumptions above that the multi-step dynamics, cost, and constraint depend continuously on **x** _t_ and **_ξ_** . Likewise, the conditions that _LNT_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) _≻_ 0 and _CNT_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** ) _⪰_ 0 follow from definition VIII.2. \n\nFinally, we observe that _V_ ( **x** _t_ ) is not only the optimal value function for the single-step problem, but also for the multi-step feedback problem when the feedback policy is parametrized by **_ξ_** _∈_ Ξ. This is shown through repeated application of Equation 11 and the assumption that the policy parameterization is rich enough to represent the optimal multi-step feedback policy: \n\n\n\nBy applying Theorem VIII.1 to **_ξ_** instead of **u** _t_ , we show _∃_ **_ξ_** _c_ s.t. ∆ _V_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** _c_ ) _<_ 0 and _CNT_ ( **x** _t,_ **_ξ_** _c_ ) = 0 which implies _c_ ( **x** _i,_ **_π_** _i_ ( **x** _i,_ **_ξ_** _c_ )) = 0 _∀ i ∈{t, ..., t_ + _NT −_ 1 _}_ . \n\nThus, this analysis provides theoretical justification for using NMPC to search for feasible feedback policies in the neighborhood of the optimal policies which descend the value function while strictly adhering to constraints. \n\n# _C. Approximate Value Functions_ \n\nThe preceding analysis assumes an optimal value function while our proposed algorithm uses learned actor and critic \n\nmodels. We acknowledge that in practice, these models converge to uncertain approximations of the optimal policy and value function. We define the expected change in the stochastic approximate value function as \n\n\n\nFor a given **x** _t_ and **u** _t_ , there is a gap between the change in the approximate and optimal value functions \n\n\n\nTo guarantee that the optimal value function, _V_ ( **x** _t_ ) actually decreases, the expected change in the approximate value function must decrease by more than this gap, \n\n\n\nGiven that _V_ ( **x** _t_ ) is unknown in practice, this gap is also unknown. Thus, we simply aim to ensure that ∆ _V_<sup>ˆ</sup> ( **x** _t,_ **u** _t_ ) _<_ 0. \n\n# _D. Simulation Experiment_ \n\nWe explore this analysis empirically using a stochastic Dubins Car dynamical system. The state is given as **x** _t_ = [ _rx ry θ_ ]<sup>_T_</sup> where _rx_ , _ry_ denote the position and _θ_ is the orientation. The input is given as **u** _t_ = [ _v θ_<sup>˙</sup> ]<sup>_T_</sup> where _v_ is the speed and _θ_<sup>˙</sup> is the angular velocity. The nominal continuous dynamics are given as **f** ( **x** _t,_ **u** _t_ ) = [ _v_ cos( _θ_ ) _, v_ sin( _θ_ ) _, θ_<sup>˙</sup> ]<sup>_T_</sup> . The stochastic, discrete-time dynamics applies Euler integration with ∆ _t_ = 0 _._ 1 sec and Gaussian process noise with **Σ** _f_ = [0 _._ 01 0 _._ 01 0 _._ 01]<sup>_T_</sup> . \n\nWe constructed a simple environment in which the goal is surrounded by dense obstacles arranged on a hexagonal lattice (Fig. 17). Initial states are sampled at positions 7 _._ 5m away from the goal with the orientation pointing towards the goal. \n\nWe trained 13 actor-critic networks with increasingly large constraint penalties, _γc_ . The inputs into these networks were the normalized position, sine/cosine of the orientation, and the normalized range and sine/cosine of the bearing to each obstacle. Each network was trained for 5 million timesteps. \n\nAcross 100 trials with differing initial states, we compared the actor policy against AC-PAC-NMPC for each _γc_ . We evaluated the percentage of trials in which the system collided with obstacles and reached the goal without colliding. Additionally, we evaluated our RL-based warm-start with and without the actor infeasibility check (lines 6-8 in Algo. 1). \n\nAs shown in Figure 18, AC-PAC-NMPC was able to successfully navigate the environment at smaller _γc_ values than the actor policy alone. This supports Theorem VIII.2 empirically and shows that for a large enough penalty, a feasible policy exists in the neighborhood of the actor policy which descends the value function. Additionally, these results show that by warm-starting with the prior policy when the actor trajectory is infeasible, the safety of AC-PAC-NMPC can be significantly improved. \n\n# IX. CONCLUSION \n\nIn this paper, we present an approach for RL-guided SNMPC to enable long-range navigation in unknown environments while enforcing constraints on probabilistic guarantees of safety. The proposed approach utilized learned actor-critic \n\n"
